---
import Container from "@/components/container.astro";
import Sectionhead from "@/components/sectionhead.astro";
---

<Container>
  <Sectionhead>
    <Fragment slot="title">Development Documentation</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="desc">
      The current CerebraSense model pipeline, training results, and deployment integration.
    </Fragment>
  </Sectionhead>

  <article class="development-content mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl">
    <section>
      <p>
        CerebraSense was upgraded from the earlier lightweight 32 x 32 custom
        CNN into a higher-resolution transfer-learning pipeline. The new
        development phase focused on improving real-world robustness,
        preserving the exact preprocessing steps used during training, and
        making the deployed model easier to version and audit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current model is an EfficientNet-B0 classifier trained on the
        original MRI image folders rather than flattened CSV-only pixel rows.
        It uses 224 x 224 image inputs, moderate MRI-safe augmentation,
        duplicate/leakage checks, class weighting, label smoothing, validation
        macro-F1 model selection, confidence calibration, and an uncertainty
        threshold for safer interpretation.
      </p>
      <p class="callout">
        The model is substantially stronger than the earlier prototype, but it
        remains a research and educational classifier. Its output is a model
        class prediction, not a clinical diagnosis.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Development Milestones</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Stage</th>
              <th>Implementation</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Baseline</td>
              <td>32 x 32 custom CNN with CSV pixel features</td>
              <td>Fast prototype and controlled architecture testing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Improved pipeline</td>
              <td>Image-folder training with 224 x 224 tensors</td>
              <td>Preserve more visual detail from MRI scans</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Transfer learning</td>
              <td>EfficientNet-B0 classifier</td>
              <td>Use a stronger pretrained visual backbone</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Robustness</td>
              <td>Augmentation, duplicate checks, class weighting</td>
              <td>Reduce overfitting and improve generalization</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Deployment</td>
              <td>Single model bundle with preprocessing metadata</td>
              <td>Keep inference consistent with notebook training</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Dataset & Preprocessing</h2>
      <p>
        The project still uses the Brain Tumor MRI Dataset by Masoud
        Nickparvar. The model classifies MRI images into four classes:
        glioma, meningioma, no tumor, and pituitary tumor.
      </p>
      <p>
        The earlier CNN converted every image into a single grayscale 32 x 32
        vector and stored it in <code>brain_tumor_train.csv</code> or
        <code>brain_tumor_test.csv</code>. The improved model trains directly
        from the folder structure under <code>data/brain_tumor_mri</code>,
        which keeps the image-loading and augmentation process closer to
        deployment.
      </p>

      <h3>Class Mapping</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th class="numeric">Index</th>
              <th>Internal Label</th>
              <th>Display Label</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">0</td>
              <td><code>glioma</code></td>
              <td>Glioma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">1</td>
              <td><code>meningioma</code></td>
              <td>Meningioma</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">2</td>
              <td><code>notumor</code></td>
              <td>No Tumor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="numeric">3</td>
              <td><code>pituitary</code></td>
              <td>Pituitary</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Dataset Counts</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Class</th>
              <th class="numeric">Training Images</th>
              <th class="numeric">Testing Images</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Glioma</td>
              <td class="numeric">1,321</td>
              <td class="numeric">300</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Meningioma</td>
              <td class="numeric">1,339</td>
              <td class="numeric">306</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>No Tumor</td>
              <td class="numeric">1,595</td>
              <td class="numeric">405</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Pituitary</td>
              <td class="numeric">1,457</td>
              <td class="numeric">300</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Total dataset size: 7,023 MRI images, with 5,712 training-source images
        and 1,311 held-out testing images.
      </p>

      <h3>Improved Preprocessing</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Images are loaded from the original class folders.</li>
        <li>EXIF orientation is normalized before processing.</li>
        <li>Images are converted to RGB for pretrained backbone compatibility.</li>
        <li>Inputs are resized to 224 x 224 pixels.</li>
        <li>ImageNet normalization is applied during training and inference.</li>
        <li>The exact preprocessing settings are saved in the deployment bundle.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Preprocessing Setting</th>
              <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Input Size</td>
              <td>224 x 224</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Color Mode</td>
              <td>RGB tensor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Normalization Mean</td>
              <td><code>[0.485, 0.456, 0.406]</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Normalization Std</td>
              <td><code>[0.229, 0.224, 0.225]</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Validation Split</td>
              <td>Stratified split from cleaned training data</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Data Quality Controls</h2>
      <p>
        The improved notebook adds image-manifest logic before training. Each
        image path is recorded with its label, split, file hash, and a simple
        perceptual hash for review.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Exact duplicate training files are removed.</li>
        <li>Exact train/test leakage is checked by SHA-256 hash.</li>
        <li>Large same-class perceptual-hash groups are reported for review.</li>
        <li>The held-out testing folder remains untouched during model selection.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These checks do not make the dataset clinically complete, but they make
        the current experiment more defensible than directly training on every
        file without leakage review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Augmentation</h2>
      <p>
        The improved model uses moderate augmentation so the model sees
        plausible MRI variation without heavily distorting anatomy.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Augmentation</th>
              <th>Configuration</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Rotation</td>
              <td>Up to 10 degrees</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Translation</td>
              <td>Up to 4 percent shift</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scale</td>
              <td>0.94 to 1.06</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Brightness / Contrast</td>
              <td>Small controlled jitter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Blur</td>
              <td>Light Gaussian blur applied occasionally</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Model Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The current CerebraSense classifier uses EfficientNet-B0 as the visual
        backbone. The pretrained classifier head is replaced with a custom
        four-class output layer for the MRI categories.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Component</th>
              <th>Current Implementation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Backbone</td>
              <td>EfficientNet-B0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Classifier</td>
              <td>Dropout 0.30, then linear layer to 4 logits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Output</td>
              <td>Glioma, meningioma, no tumor, pituitary</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Deployment Version</td>
              <td><code>efficientnet-b0-transfer-calibrated-v1</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The notebook supports alternative backbones such as ResNet18 and
        MobileNetV3 Small, but EfficientNet-B0 is the selected deployment
        model because it delivered strong accuracy while remaining compact
        enough for the current model service.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Training Configuration</h2>
      <p>
        The model was trained with a stronger optimization setup than the
        earlier prototype. The validation checkpoint is selected by macro F1,
        which helps prevent the best model from favoring only the easiest or
        largest class.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Setting</th>
              <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Optimizer</td>
              <td>AdamW</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Initial Learning Rate</td>
              <td>3e-4</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Weight Decay</td>
              <td>1e-4</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Loss Function</td>
              <td>Cross-entropy with class weights and label smoothing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Label Smoothing</td>
              <td>0.05</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Batch Size</td>
              <td>32</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Requested Epochs</td>
              <td>30</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Model Selection</td>
              <td>Validation macro F1, with accuracy as a secondary signal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Random Seed</td>
              <td>42</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Evaluation Results</h2>
      <p>
        The improved model exceeded the previous 97.33 percent CNN benchmark.
        It reached 98.93 percent accuracy and 98.89 percent macro F1 on the
        held-out test folder.
      </p>

      <div class="metric-grid">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span>Validation Accuracy</span>
